# Test, coverage and profiling output
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
coverage.out
coverage.html
*.coverprofile
cpu.prof
mem.prof
block.prof
mutex.prof
trace.out

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Fuzzing
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# There are two kinds of file under testdata/fuzz/<FuzzTarget>/ and they must be
# treated in opposite ways.
#
#   1. The SEED CORPUS: files a human wrote and named, one per interesting case.
#      These are source. They are checked in, they are reviewed, and every new
#      record type must add one (see CONTRIBUTING.md). Nothing below ignores
#      them, and nothing should.
#
#   2. FUZZER ARTEFACTS: files `go test -fuzz` writes itself when it finds a
#      failing input. Go names these after the SHA-256 digest of their contents,
#      so they are exactly 64 lower-case hexadecimal characters with no
#      extension. A 30-second local fuzz run can drop several of these into the
#      working tree, and committing them by reflex is how a corpus turns into
#      landfill.
#
# The pattern below matches (2) and not (1), because a descriptive seed name
# always contains a character outside [0-9a-f] within its first sixteen. When a
# fuzzer artefact turns out to be a genuine regression worth keeping, rename it
# to describe the defect and `git add` it like any other seed.
#
# The leading `**/` is load-bearing: a pattern containing a slash is anchored to
# this file's directory, so without it only a testdata/ directory at the
# repository root would match, and every per-package corpus would be missed.
**/testdata/fuzz/*/[0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f][0-9a-f]*

# The generated corpus the fuzzing engine keeps between runs lives in the build
# cache, not here, but an explicit -fuzzcachedir can redirect it into the tree.
/fuzzcache/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Go tooling
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Workspaces are a local development convenience and are never shared: a
# committed go.work silently overrides every collaborator's module resolution.
go.work
go.work.sum

